goldbook:00372
    a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:identifier "00372"^^xsd:token ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:provenance <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00372.ttl> ;
    dcterms:rightsHolder "International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC)" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00372.ttl> ;
    skos:definition "Originally the thermodynamic preference for polar groups bonded to C-1 (the anomeric carbon of a glycopyranosyl derivative) to take up an axial position.  This effect is now considered to be a special case of a general preference (the generalized anomeric effect) for synclinal (gauche) conformations about the bond C–Y in the system X–C–Y–C where X and Y are heteroatoms having nonbonding electron pairs, commonly at least one of which is nitrogen, oxygen or fluorine. For example in chloro(methoxy)methane the anomeric effect stabilizes the synclinal conformation.  In alkyl glycopyranosides the anomeric effect operates at two sites (i) along the endocyclic C-1 oxygen bond (endo-anomeric effect) and (ii) along the exocyclic C-1 oxygen bond (exo-anomeric effect). The opposite preference is claimed for some systems, e.g., glycopyranosyltrialkylammonium salts, and has been referred to as the reverse anomeric effect."@en ;
    skos:inScheme goldbook: ;
    skos:prefLabel "anomeric effect"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf goldbook: ;
    prov:hadPrimarySource <https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.A00372> ;
.

goldbook:00373
    a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:identifier "00373"^^xsd:token ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:provenance <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00373.ttl> ;
    dcterms:rightsHolder "International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC)" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00373.ttl> ;
    skos:definition "Diastereoisomers of glycosides, hemiacetals or related cyclic forms of sugars, or related molecules differing in configuration only at C-1 of an aldose, C-2 of a 2-ketose, etc."@en ;
    skos:inScheme goldbook: ;
    skos:prefLabel "anomers"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf goldbook: ;
    prov:hadPrimarySource <https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.A00373> ;
.

goldbook:00374
    a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:identifier "00374"^^xsd:token ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:provenance <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00374.ttl> ;
    dcterms:rightsHolder "International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC)" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00374.ttl> ;
    skos:definition "Benzene derivatives having para positions (or meta) bridged by a chain (commonly 10 to 12 atoms long) (Latin ansa, handle). By extension, any arene bridged by a chain constrained to lie over one of the two faces of the arene."@en ;
    skos:inScheme goldbook: ;
    skos:prefLabel "ansa compounds"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf goldbook: ;
    prov:hadPrimarySource <https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.A00374> ;
.

goldbook:00375
    a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:identifier "00375"^^xsd:token ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:provenance <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00375.ttl> ;
    dcterms:rightsHolder "International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC)" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00375.ttl> ;
    skos:definition "Combined effect of two or more factors which is smaller than the solitary effect of any one of those factors. In bioassays, the term may be used when a specified response is produced by exposure to either of two factors but not by exposure to both together."@en ;
    skos:inScheme goldbook: ;
    skos:prefLabel "antagonism"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf goldbook: ;
    prov:hadPrimarySource <https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.A00375> ;
.

goldbook:00376
    a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:identifier "00376"^^xsd:token ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:provenance <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00376.ttl> ;
    dcterms:rightsHolder "International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC)" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00376.ttl> ;
    skos:definition "Substance that reverses or reduces the effect induced by an agonist.Substance that attaches to and blocks cell receptors that normally bind naturally occurring substances."@en ;
    skos:inScheme goldbook: ;
    skos:prefLabel "antagonist"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf goldbook: ;
    prov:hadPrimarySource <https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.A00376> ;
.

goldbook:00377
    a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:identifier "00377"^^xsd:token ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:provenance <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00377.ttl> ;
    dcterms:rightsHolder "International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC)" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/nfdi4cat/goldbook-skos/blame/main/vocabularies/goldbook/IDs00xxx/00377.ttl> ;
    skos:definition "When a part of a molecule ('molecular fragment') undergoes two changes in bonding (bond-making or bond-breaking), either to a common centre or to two related centres, external to itself, these bonding changes may be related in one of two spatially different ways. These are designated as 'antarafacial' if opposite faces of the molecular fragment are involved, and 'suprafacial' if both changes occur at the same face. The concept of 'face' is clear from the diagrams in the cases of planar (or approximately planar) frameworks with isolated or interacting p-orbitals [diagrams (a) and (b)].  The terms antarafacial and suprafacial are, however, also employed in cases in which the essential part of the molecular fragment undergoing changes in bonding comprises two atoms linked only by a $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$. In these cases it is customary to refer to the phases of the local $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonding}$ orbital: occurrence of the two bonding changes at sites of like orbital phase is regarded as suprafacial, whereas that at two sites of opposite phase is antarafacial. The possibilities are shown for $\\ce{C–C}$ and $\\ce{C–H}$ $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bonds}$ in diagrams (c) and (d). There may be two distinct and alternative stereochemical outcomes of a suprafacial process involving a $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ between saturated carbon atoms, i.e. either retention or inversion at both centres. The antarafacial process results in inversion at one centre and retention at the second. For examples of the use of these terms, see cycloaddition, sigmatropic rearrangement."@en ;
    skos:inScheme goldbook: ;
    skos:prefLabel "antarafacial"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf goldbook: ;
    prov:hadPrimarySource <https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.A00377> ;
.

goldbook:00379
    a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:identifier "00379"^^xsd:token ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-11-04"^^xsd:date ;
